+++
title: Middle Assyrian Empire
description: Third period of Assyrian history
key-facts:
  - name: First king
    value: Ashur-uballit I, who came to the throne c. 1363 BC
  - name: Last king
    value: Ashur-dan II, who died in 912 BC
  - name: Capital
    value: Assur (c. 1363–1233 BC), then Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta (c. 1233–1207 BC),
      then Assur again (c. 1207–912 BC)
  - name: Government
    value: A monarchy
  - name: Languages spoken
    value: Akkadian, Hurrian, Amorite and Aramaic
  - name: Land it covered
    value: Parts of what is now Iraq, Syria, Turkey and Iran
image-plan:
  - file: Amarna_letter-_Royal_Letter_from_Ashur-uballit,_the_king_of_Assyria,_to_the_king_of_Egypt_MET_24.2.11_EGDP021806.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Amarna letter EA 15, sent by Ashur-uballit I of Assyria (r. c.
      1363–1328 BC) to Akhenaten of Egypt (r. c. 1353–1336) — the king who
      claimed the dignity of a great king on the level of the pharaohs.
  - file: Arranging_the_boundary_between_Assyria_and_Babylon.png
    role: inline
    section: formation-and-rise
    caption: Assyrian–Babylonian border negotiations in a 20th-century
      illustration; in the centuries after Ashur-uballit, Assyrian kings often
      found themselves rivals of the Babylonian kings.
  - file: Ancient_Near_East_1300BC.svg
    role: full
    section: formation-and-rise
    caption: "The Ancient Near East in 1300 BC, approximately: the world in which
      Assyria secured its independence from Mitanni."
  - file: Shalmaneser_I_pours_out_the_dust_of_Arina_before_his_god_Ashur.jpg
    role: inline
    section: reigns-of-adad-nirari-i-and-shalmaneser-i
    caption: Shalmaneser I (r. c. 1273–1244 BC) pours out the dust of Arinnu, the
      fortress he razed to the ground; some of the dust was collected and
      symbolically brought back to Assur. A 20th-century illustration.
  - file: Image_from_page_33_of_"Ancient_seals_of_the_Near_East"_(1940).jpg
    role: inline
    section: reigns-of-adad-nirari-i-and-shalmaneser-i
    caption: A seal from the Middle Assyrian Empire, c. 1400–1100 BC.
  - file: Moyen_Orient_13e_siècle-es.svg
    role: full
    section: reign-of-tukulti-ninurta-i
    caption: The Ancient Near East in the 13th century BC, when the Middle
      Assyrian Empire was at its height. Babylonia in the south was an
      Assyrian vassal c. 1225–1216 BC.
  - file: Detail._Assyrian_king_Tukulti-Ninurta_I_stands_and_kneels,_13th_century_BCE._From_Assur,_Iraq._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: reign-of-tukulti-ninurta-i
    caption: Tukulti-Ninurta I (r. c. 1243–1207 BC), depicted both standing and
      kneeling.
  - file: Ashurreshishi.png
    role: inline
    section: first-period-of-decline
    caption: The empire under Ashur-resh-ishi I (r. 1132–1115 BC), who claimed the
      epithet "avenger of Assyria".
  - file: Relief_of_Tiglath-Pileser_I.png
    role: inline
    section: second-period-of-expansion-and-consolidation
    caption: Tiglath-Pileser I (r. 1114–1076 BC), whose campaigns reached as far
      as the Mediterranean.
  - file: Terracotta_octagon_of_the_Assyrian_king_Tiglath-pileser_I,_1110_BCE,_from_Assur,_Iraq._British_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: second-period-of-expansion-and-consolidation
    caption: A terracotta octagon from Assur recounting the campaigns and civil
      activities of Tiglath-Pileser I.
  - file: Stele_of_Ashur-resh-ishi_II,_972–967_BCE._From_Assur,_Iraq._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: century-of-crisis
    caption: Stele of Ashur-resh-ishi II (r. 972–967 BC) from Assur, from the
      poorly documented century of Assyrian crisis.
  - file: Assyria_in_reign_of_Adad-nirari_II.jpg
    role: inline
    section: beginning-of-the-assyrian-reconquista
    caption: Assyrian borders and campaigns under Ashur-dan II (r. 934–912 BC),
      whose reign ended the second period of decline, and under the first
      Neo-Assyrian kings.
  - file: Fragment_de_fresque_de_Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta_AO_19226.jpg
    role: inline
    section: royal-palaces-and-attendants
    caption: A fragment of wall painting from Tukulti-Ninurta I's royal palace at
      Kar-Tukulti-Ninurta.
  - file: Stele_of_Ashur-mudammeq,_governor_of_Ninevah._1200_BCE,_from_Assur,_Iraq._Pergamon_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: royal-administration-and-provincial-framework
    caption: Stele of Ashur-mudammeq, governor of Nineveh, c. 1200 BC; each
      province was headed by a governor responsible for the local economy and
      public order.
  - file: Middle_Assyrian_cuneiform_tablet_-_administrative_memorandum.png
    role: inline
    section: non-provincial-territories
    caption: An administrative memorandum on a 13th-century BC Middle Assyrian
      cuneiform tablet.
  - file: Middle_Assyrian_cuneiform_tablet_-_administrative_record.png
    role: inline
    section: taxation-and-recruitment
    caption: A 13th-century BC Middle Assyrian cuneiform tablet holding an
      administrative record.
  - file: Adad-nirari_I's_sword.png
    role: inline
    section: military
    caption: A ceremonial sickle sword which once belonged to Adad-nirari I.
  - file: Middle_Assyrian_winged_horse_cylinder_seal.png
    role: inline
    section: social-classes
    caption: A Middle Assyrian cylinder seal depicting a winged horse, with a
      modern impression alongside it.
  - file: Receipt_of_sheep_and_goat,_fees_for_religious_purification_conducted_by_the_exorcist_Res-Marduk_for_Remine,_wife_of_the_Assyrian_king_Ninurta-Tukulti-Assur._From_Assur,_Iraq._Middle_Assyrian_period,_c._1134_BCE._Ancient_Orient_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: families-and-position-of-women
    caption: A 12th-century BC receipt for a sheep and a goat, payment for a
      religious purification ritual conducted by an exorcist.
  - file: Incense_burner._Hurrian_period,_1300-1000_BC._From_Tell_Basmosian,_Dukan,_Iraq._Erbil_Civilization_Museum.jpg
    role: inline
    section: ethnic-groups
    caption: A Hurrian incense burner from Lake Dukan, c. 1300–1000 BC; the
      Hurrians were among the most prominent foreign groups within the empire.
  - file: Middle_Assyrian_road_system_map.png
    role: full
    section: road-system
    caption: The Middle Assyrian road system, approximately. The roads in purple
      are archaeologically attested; those in red are also likely to have
      existed.
  - file: Temple_altar_offered_by_Tukulti-Ninurta_I._1243-1207_BCE._From_Assur,_Iraq._Ancient_Orient_Museum,_Istanbul.jpg
    role: inline
    section: religion
    caption: A temple altar from Assur, made in the reign of Tukulti-Ninurta I.
  - file: Middle_Assyrian_seal_with_cultic_motif.png
    role: inline
    section: religion
    caption: The impression of a Middle Assyrian cylinder seal, depicting a
      religious scene.
further:
  - slug: old-assyrian-period
    why: "The city-state Assyria was before: ruled with the title //iššiak//
      jointly with Assur's city assembly, and home to the merchant families
      whose descendants became the Middle Assyrian elite."
  - slug: neo-assyrian-empire
    why: What came next, and by the article's own account the direct continuation
      of the same civilization — its early kings described their wars as
      reconquests of what the Middle Assyrian Empire had lost.
  - slug: early-assyrian-period
    why: Where Ashur began, as a deified personification of the city of Assur
      itself, centuries before he was made a god of war.
